; One variable: a roughness map on an external `.tres` material. Every other roughness fixture
; uses a `[sub_resource]` material, and every `.tres` fixture carries scalars alone, so a
; regression here turns this surface into the default material (mid-grey, roughness 0.8) or a
; flat `roughness = 1.0` while every other scene stays green.
[gd_scene load_steps=3 format=3]

; The map splits glossy (0.10) over rough (0.90), sampled raw as a non-colour map. The upper
; cap shows a small hard highlight, the lower a broad dim one, with a sharp seam. Without the
; map both caps are the same dull matte. Bound to a colour slot, the albedo goes banded.
[ext_resource type="StandardMaterial3D" path="res://unit-external-material-roughness.tres" id="1_mat"]

[sub_resource type="SphereMesh" id="Sphere_1"]
radius = 1.0
height = 2.0

; `metallic = 0`, so the sky's reflection is the 0.04 Fresnel floor and the picture is the
; direct light's specular lobe, not a mirrored environment no roughness map could be judged
; through. Authoring a DirectionalLight3D removes Godot's editor preview sun (ADR-0025) and
; keeps its preview environment.
[node name="ExternalMaterialRoughness" type="Node3D"]

; Basis rows point local -Z, the direction the light travels, down and forward at 40 degrees.
; The specular highlight then lands inside the glossy band, clear of the seam, so the two caps
; read against each other. `light_energy = 2.0` is an ordinary brightness that a dark albedo
; resolves well short of clipping, so no roughness signal is lost in a saturated highlight.
[node name="Sun" type="DirectionalLight3D" parent="."]
transform = Transform3D(1, 0, 0, 0, 0.766044, 0.642788, 0, -0.642788, 0.766044, 0, 3, 3)
light_energy = 2.0

[node name="Ball" type="MeshInstance3D" parent="."]
mesh = SubResource("Sphere_1")
surface_material_override/0 = ExtResource("1_mat")
